      "annotatedScript": "[STILLNESS] At 4:12 p.m. on the third Tuesday of October, the fog in the North Cascades did not roll in\u2014it dropped like a wet wool blanket over five thousand eight hundred feet of granite and subalpine fir. [PAUSE 1.5s] Caleb Vance knelt on a slick shelf of shale, his bare knuckles purple from the sleet, and held a magnifying loupe two inches above a snapped twig of mountain heather. The break was [EMPHASIS: two millimeters wide]. To the twenty-two search-and-rescue volunteers sweeping the drainage below him, it was nothing. To Caleb, it was a sentence written in fresh sap. [BEAT]\n\nCaleb was fifty-four, a former wildlife tracker for the Washington Department of Fish and Wildlife who had spent thirty years following wolverines, lynx, and lost elk across terrain that erased most human footprints in ten minutes. His routine never changed: wool base layer, waxed canvas coat, a brass compass older than his truck, and a silence so complete that birds forgot he was there. [SCENE SHIFT] Two days earlier, a twenty-nine-year-old alpine botanist named Tessa Morales had hiked up the Hart's Pass spur to catalog late-season sedges before the first hard freeze. She had a satellite beacon, a down jacket, and four years of backcountry experience. None of it had stopped the early storm from turning the switchbacks into black ice. Her last ping had come from the edge of Slate Creek basin at noon on Sunday. Now it was Tuesday afternoon, the temperature was dropping toward twenty-six degrees, and the county sheriff's office was forty minutes from calling off the ground grid until dawn. [PAUSE 2s]\n\n[SPEED UP] Down at the makeshift command tarp, the air smelled of wet nylon, diesel generator exhaust, and cold coffee. Caleb's boots squelched as he approached the folding table where Incident Commander Ray Halvorsen was tapping a stylus against a waterproof tablet. [LEAN IN] \"The thermal drone cleared the lower ravine twenty minutes ago,\" Ray said, not looking up. \"Nothing. No heat signature, no bright fabric, no tracks on the creekbed. We're pulling the flankers back to the trailhead before dark.\" Caleb set his loupe on the map, right over a contour line that curved away from the water. \"She's not in the ravine, Ray,\" Caleb said. [LOWER VOICE] \"She went up the windbreak.\"\n\nRay finally looked up, his jaw tight beneath a three-day stubble. \"Caleb, nobody climbs into a forty-knot crosswind when they're hypothermic. Gravity pulls people down. Every manual in that truck says lost hikers follow water.\" \"Manuals are written for people who panic,\" Caleb replied. \"Tessa's a botanist. She knows the creekbed floods with freezing mist at dusk. Look at the heather above the 5,600-foot line.\" Ray sighed, rubbing his temples. \"I've got twenty-two tired people, three twisted ankles, and forty minutes of usable light. I can't send a line up a sheer scree field because you saw a broken bush.\" [PAUSE 1.5s] \"It wasn't a bush,\" Caleb said quietly. \"It was a pattern.\" [BEAT]\n\n[SCENE SHIFT] [SLOW DOWN] Ten minutes later, Caleb was alone again, climbing back toward the shale shelf while the rest of the team packed their packs below. The sleet had turned to granular snow that hissed against his hood. His left knee\u2014the one he had torn on a timberline fall six winters ago\u2014ached with every step, and for the first time all afternoon, doubt crept in beside the cold. [LOWER VOICE] Maybe Ray was right. Maybe the wind had snapped that heather. Maybe Caleb was just an old man who couldn't admit that the mountain had beaten him, that thermal cameras and algorithms had made his eyes obsolete. His headlamp beam barely pierced six feet into the gray soup. He stopped, breathing hard, watching his own vapor vanish into the dark. [PAUSE 2s]\n\nThen he forced himself to stop looking for a person and start looking for [EMPHASIS: what a person costs the landscape]. He dropped to one knee beside a stunted subalpine fir. On the leeward side, three feet off the ground, a single dry needle cluster had been brushed\u2014not snapped, just rotated twenty degrees out of its natural growth angle. Ten feet higher, a pebble the size of a thumbnail sat dry-side-up in a patch of wet moss. [SPEED UP] Someone had stepped there within the last twelve hours, moving deliberately, conserving energy, stepping on rock to avoid mud. [BEAT] Caleb's radio crackled on his shoulder. \"Vance, this is Halvorsen. Wheels up at the trailhead in fifteen. Come down.\" Caleb keyed the mic. \"Negative, Ray. Give me twenty minutes. Tell the medic to warm up the IV bags.\" \"Caleb, don't be a hero\u2014\" Caleb clicked the radio off. [PAUSE 1.5s]\n\n[SCENE SHIFT] He followed the invisible thread upward. At 6,100 feet, the wind howled across a saddle of shattered basalt, stripping the sound from his own voice. He didn't shout. Shouting wasted breath and made people listen for echoes instead of looking at the ground. Instead, he swept his headlamp low, three inches above the lichen. [LEAN IN] There\u2014a snapped lupine stem, bent toward the north. There\u2014a scrape of Vibram rubber no wider than a fingernail on a slate edge. [EMPHASIS: The forest keeps receipts], Caleb's mentor had told him thirty years ago, back when he was a rookie who couldn't tell a deer bed from a windfall. [LOWER VOICE] \"You just have to read the handwriting.\"\n\nTwenty yards past the basalt saddle, tucked behind a granite overhang that blocked ninety percent of the gale, Caleb's beam caught a flash of dull silver. Not bright orange. Not a flare. Just the edge of a crinkled Mylar emergency blanket wedged between two boulders. [PAUSE 2s] Tessa Morales was curled inside, conscious but shivering so violently her teeth clicked like castanets. Her left ankle was swollen to the size of a grapefruit, wrapped tightly in her own scarf. When Caleb knelt beside her and clicked his headlamp to red so he wouldn't blind her, she stared at him for three full seconds before speaking. \"How?\" she whispered, her lips blue. \"The drone flew right over me twice. I couldn't stand up to wave.\" [WARM SMILE] Caleb unclipped his insulated thermos of hot broth and pressed it into her gloved hands. [LOWER VOICE] \"Thermal drones see heat, not intent,\" he said. \"You broke a two-millimeter twig on your way up.\"\n\n[SCENE SHIFT] [SLOW DOWN] By 6:30 p.m., six volunteers with a wheeled litter had reached the overhang, guided by Caleb's strobe. The descent took three hours in the dark, every step negotiated over ice, until the ambulance doors finally thudded shut at the trailhead and the red taillights dissolved into the pines. [BEAT] Ray Halvorsen stood beside Caleb's truck, holding two paper cups of lukewarm coffee. Neither man drank right away. \"Twenty-two people,\" Ray said, staring at the dark ridge above them. \"Four drones. Three dogs. And you found her off a broken twig.\" Caleb looked down at his muddy boots, then up at the fog that had already swallowed the trail again. [PAUSE 1.5s] \"Everybody was looking for a rescue,\" Caleb said. \"I was just looking for [EMPHASIS: the quietest signal in the dark].\" [STILLNESS] [PAUSE 2s]",
