      "annotatedScript": "[SLOW DOWN] At eleven-forty on a Tuesday night in November, the fourth-floor cardiac waiting room at Mercy General Hospital in Toledo smelled of burnt Folgers, wet wool, and floor wax. [PAUSE 1.5s] Outside the double-paned windows, sleet ticked against the glass like dry rice. Inside, the only steady sound was the low, mechanical hum of a corner vending machine whose fluorescent tube flickered every fourteen seconds. \n\nMiriam Okafor, fifty-four years old and fourteen hours into her shift as staff chaplain, stood by the nurse's station with a clipboard tucked under her arm. Her routine at this hour was simple: check the overnight census, walk the quiet corridors, and sit with whoever could not sleep. [LOWER VOICE] She had been doing it for eleven years. She knew the difference between ordinary grief and the kind of silence that could crack a room in half. \n\n[SCENE SHIFT] Tonight, the silence belonged to two men sitting at opposite ends of a vinyl sofa. \n\nDavid Mercer, forty-eight, a commercial roofing estimator, wore a damp Carhartt jacket and stared at his boots. Twelve feet away, his younger brother, Thomas, forty-five, a regional logistics manager, sat in a wrinkled Oxford shirt with his wool overcoat folded neatly across his knees. Between them lay three empty chairs and [EMPHASIS: nine years of practiced silence]. \n\nBehind the frosted doors of Room 412, their father, Arthur Mercer, was fighting through the aftermath of a massive coronary. The charge nurse had just whispered to Miriam that Dr. Sanjay Kulkarni was scrubbing out and would be walking into the waiting room within minutes to deliver the prognosis. [LEAN IN] Miriam knew what happened when a doctor walked into a fractured family. One brother would hear the numbers; the other would hear the blame. And once that door opened, she would lose her chance. She had [EMPHASIS: less than ninety seconds] to get them in the same room\u2014not just physically, but emotionally\u2014before the verdict arrived. \n\n[SPEED UP] She walked over, pulled a plastic chair three feet from the sofa, and sat down. \"Gentlemen,\" she said softly. \"I'm Chaplain Okafor. How are you both holding up?\" \n\n[BEAT] Neither man looked up. \n\nDavid's jaw tightened. \"I'm fine,\" he said to the linoleum. \n\nThomas adjusted the cuff of his shirt. \"We're waiting on the doctor. That's all.\" \n\nMiriam tried again, leaning forward. \"I know nights like this stretch on. Sometimes it helps just to talk through what your dad's been like these last few months\u2014\" \n\n\"Look,\" David cut in, his voice flat and rough. \"With respect, ma'am, you don't know this family. Nine years ago, my brother decided he was too busy for a roofing truck and too smart for a small town. He hasn't been in Toledo since Mom's funeral. So whatever speech you've got ready, save it.\" \n\nThomas finally turned his head, his eyes sharp behind rimless glasses. \"That's a convenient version of history, David. You're the one who changed the locks on the garage before the probate papers were even dry.\" \n\n\"Because you were trying to sell the house out from under him!\" \n\n\"I was trying to keep the bank from taking it!\" \n\n[STILLNESS] [PAUSE 1.5s] The words hung in the fluorescent air. Down the hall, a monitor beeped twice and settled. Miriam did not raise her voice. She did not lecture them about forgiveness or remind them that their father was seventy-six years old and running out of time. She had seen this exact wall a hundred times. A long pastoral speech would bounce right off it. \n\n[LOWER VOICE] She stood up, walked back to the nurse's station, and leaned against the counter for ten seconds, rubbing the bridge of her nose. Her feet ached. Her shift had ended forty minutes ago. For a brief, sharp moment, she thought about letting Dr. Kulkarni handle it alone. She was tired of carrying other people's wreckage. \n\nThen the door to Room 412 clicked\u2014not open yet, just the nurse's badge scanning from the inside. Thirty seconds. Maybe forty. \n\n[LEAN IN] Miriam looked back at the two men. [EMPHASIS: Nobody listens to a lecture in a waiting room.] If you want to silence a restless room, you do not start with your conclusion. You start with a disruption so specific, so undeniably human, that the armor drops before the mind can argue. \n\nShe reached into her cardigan pocket. Her fingers found a single quarter, warm from her palm. \n\n[SCENE SHIFT] She walked past the sofa, straight to the humming vending machine in the corner. She dropped the quarter into the slot\u2014[EMPHASIS: clink]\u2014pressed B-4, and waited as the spiral turned. A can dropped with a heavy thud. She reached into her other pocket, found a second quarter, and pressed B-4 again. \n\nBoth brothers were watching her now, puzzled by the noise. \n\nMiriam walked back to the low laminate table between them. [SLOW DOWN] Without saying a word, she set [EMPHASIS: two warm cans of ginger ale] side by side on the table. \n\n[PAUSE 2s] \n\nDavid blinked. He stared at the green-and-gold aluminum cans. Slowly, Thomas uncrossed his ankles. \n\n\"Vernors,\" Thomas murmured, barely audible. \n\nMiriam did not sit down. She stood at the edge of the table, her hands clasped loosely in front of her. [LOWER VOICE] \"Your dad's chart says he's been asking for it since six o'clock,\" she said. \"The nurse told him he couldn't have any until morning. He said, 'Tell my boys to check the glovebox of the blue Chevy\u2014there's always a spare.'\" \n\n[PAUSE 1.5s] \n\nDavid let out a short, broken breath that sounded halfway between a laugh and a sob. \"He sold that Chevy in 2011,\" he said quietly. \n\n\"He still thinks it's in the driveway,\" Thomas said. His voice had lost its edge. He looked across the three empty chairs at his older brother for the first time all night. \"Remember when he made us push it up Cherry Street in the snow because he wouldn't pay for a tow?\" \n\nDavid's shoulders dropped an inch. \"You steered. I pushed. My boots were soaked for three days.\" \n\n\"You're the one who wouldn't let me push,\" Thomas said. \n\nMiriam stepped back two paces, blending into the shadow of the doorframe just as the frosted doors of [EMPHASIS: the door to Room 412] swung open. Dr. Kulkarni stepped out in his blue scrubs, clipboard in hand, scanning the room for the family. \n\n[WARM SMILE] When he looked toward the vinyl sofa, David and Thomas were no longer twelve feet apart. They had moved to the center cushions, shoulder to shoulder, one of them holding a warm can of ginger ale he had not even opened yet. \n\n[SLOW DOWN] Miriam did not say another word. She simply nodded to the doctor, turned toward the stairwell, and went home. [PAUSE 2s] Nine years of silence had not been solved in a minute. It had been interrupted\u2014by a quarter, a memory, and a woman who knew that the right opening never asks for permission.",
