      "annotatedScript": "[STILLNESS] [LOWER VOICE] At four-oh-five on a Tuesday morning in October, the air inside Morales Hearth & Crumb on Shrewsbury Street in Worcester smelled of toasted rye, damp stone, and diesel exhaust creeping under the loading-bay door. [PAUSE 1.5s] Tomas Morales stood beside the thirty-quart Hobart mixer with a clipboard wedged under his forearm, watching his nephew Mateo dust the wooden peels with coarse cornmeal. Tomas was fifty-eight, his knuckles permanently chalked white from thirty-one years of folding dough before sunrise. [SPEED UP] Every Tuesday followed the same choreography: four hundred sourdough boules, two hundred seeded rye loaves, and three hundred sixty sandwich rolls stacked into blue plastic crates for Baystate Grocers\u2014the regional supermarket chain that had kept the bakery's lights on since 2014. [SLOW DOWN] That single account represented [EMPHASIS: forty-two percent of the ovens] every single week. Tomas had built the entire morning schedule around Baystate's five-thirty pickup truck. [BEAT] At four-eighteen, the office printer by the flour scale clicked once and spat out a single sheet of paper. Tomas didn't look up at first; he was checking the steam-injection gauge on the deck oven. When he finally walked over and lifted the page, he saw the Baystate Grocers procurement header. Effective Friday, the email read, regional bread sourcing was consolidating to an automated facility in Hartford. [PAUSE 2s] No transition period. No ninety-day wind-down. Just three paragraphs of polite corporate prose ending a twelve-year relationship in seventy-two hours.\n\n[SCENE SHIFT] By six-thirty, Tomas had already made three phone calls from the cramped back office, sitting on an overturned five-gallon shortening bucket because the swivel chair's caster had snapped in August. [SPEED UP] His first call went to Dan Kowalski, the regional buyer he had known for nine years. Dan didn't pick up. His second call went to the central procurement desk, where a brisk assistant named Priya told him the decision was final and already signed off by the Hartford VP. [LEAN IN] \"Tomas,\" she said evenly, \"the new vendor is delivering at sixty-eight cents a loaf less than your wholesale sheet. We can't match that from a single-site kitchen.\" [PAUSE 1.5s] Tomas stared at the grease-stained wall calendar. \"Priya,\" he replied, \"we're baking with stone-milled flour from Vermont. Our loaves don't sit in a warehouse for four days.\" There was a brief crackle on the line. \"Our shoppers aren't reading the flour bag at six p.m.,\" she said. \"They're looking at the yellow price tag.\" [LOWER VOICE] When the line went dead, Tomas walked back into the main room. Mateo was sliding the last rack of Baystate rye onto the cooling trolley. Tomas didn't tell him yet. Instead, he drove twenty minutes across town to a mid-tier restaurant distributor he had turned down two years earlier, hoping they still needed a local bread partner. The warehouse manager, a tall man in a quilted vest, shook his head before Tomas even finished his pitch. \"You're too expensive for our diners, Tomas. And you're too small for our hospitals.\"\n\n[SCENE SHIFT] [SLOW DOWN] That night, at eleven-forty, the bakery was dead silent except for the low hum of the retarder-proofer cabinet. Tomas sat alone at the stainless-steel prep table with a yellow legal pad and a stubby carpenter's pencil. [STILLNESS] He wrote down the Friday payroll: six full-time bakers, two part-time counter staff, and his daughter Elena, who handled the books after her evening shift at the hospital. Then he wrote down the rent on the Shrewsbury Street storefront, the natural gas bill for the deck ovens, and the monthly note on the delivery van. [LOWER VOICE] Without Baystate Grocers, the math didn't bend\u2014it snapped. He had enough cash in the checking account for three weeks of payroll, maybe four if he delayed the flour invoice. [PAUSE 2s] For the first time in thirty-one years, Tomas felt the specific, cold humiliation of realizing that his loyalty to one big buyer had quietly hollowed out everything else. He had turned down wedding orders, skipped the weekend farmers' market, and stopped testing new pastries because Baystate's blue crates demanded every square inch of rack space. [BEAT] At midnight, his phone buzzed on the steel table. It was a text from his landlord: a polite reminder that the triple-net property tax adjustment was due on the first of the month. Tomas set the phone face down. He looked at the dark glass of the front window, watching a wet October sleet begin to streak the streetlight outside, and wondered how he was going to tell his family at five in the morning that the bakery might not see Thanksgiving.\n\n[SCENE SHIFT] [LEAN IN] At five-fifteen a.m., Elena walked through the back door wearing her navy scrubs, carrying two paper cups of black coffee. She didn't ask why her father was still in yesterday's flour-dusted apron; she saw the printed Baystate email sitting beside the legal pad. [PAUSE 1.5s] She read it twice, set the coffee down, and pulled a stool up to the prep table. \"How much did we actually make on each Baystate loaf after fuel, crates, and returns?\" she asked. Tomas blinked. \"Gross margin was thirty-two percent.\" Elena shook her head. [EMPHASIS: \"Not gross, Dad. Net.\"] She opened her laptop, pulled up the third-quarter ledger, and turned the screen toward him. \"You paid Mateo overtime every Monday and Thursday to load those crates. You ate four percent in stale returns every week because their shelf-life policy penalized us. [SLOW DOWN] Once you count the van's transmission repair and the weekend labor, [EMPHASIS: the margin was never in the crate.] We were clearing eleven cents a loaf.\" Tomas stared at the spreadsheet, his jaw tight. \"Eleven cents times nine hundred loaves a week is still rent,\" he said. \"No,\" Elena replied quietly. \"It's a trap that kept you too tired to look at the front door.\" She pointed toward the retail counter, where a faded chalkboard listed only four items. \"Yesterday, a woman from the medical center across the street bought two rosemary focaccias at eight dollars each and asked if we did lunch boxes for thirty people. You told her we didn't have the oven space.\" [PAUSE 2s] Tomas looked at the empty blue crates stacked against the wall. For twelve years, he had treated the front counter like a hobby and the loading dock like a lifeline.\n\n[SCENE SHIFT] [WARM SMILE] By seven o'clock that morning, Tomas didn't call another grocery buyer. Instead, he walked across Shrewsbury Street in the cold rain carrying a warm tray of rosemary focaccia and six seeded sourdough loaves sliced thick. [SPEED UP] He walked into the lobby of the medical center's administrative wing, asked for the catering coordinator, and left the tray on the reception desk with a handwritten card: *Fresh from the four-a.m. oven. Ask about Friday lunch.* [BEAT] By noon, he had three standing orders for hospital department meetings. By Friday\u2014the exact day the Baystate truck didn't show up\u2014Mateo was loading smaller, insulated boxes into the van for four local coffee shops and two independent Italian delis within three miles of the bakery. [SLOW DOWN] They baked half as many loaves that first month, but they sold every single one at full retail or direct-to-cafe wholesale, with zero stale returns and zero five-thirty-a.m. loading-dock penalties. [STILLNESS] Six months later, Tomas stood at the same Hobart mixer at four-oh-five in the morning, listening to the rain against the brick. Baystate Grocers was gone, replaced by nineteen neighborhood accounts that knew his name. [LOWER VOICE] He never put the blue plastic crates back on the floor. He stacked them behind the dumpster, because [EMPHASIS: they bought the crust, not the story]\u2014and the story was finally his to sell. [PAUSE 2s]",
