      "annotatedScript": "[STILLNESS] At eleven-forty-two on a Tuesday night in November, the only sound on Sycamore Street was the rain hitting the tin awning of the Millbrook Chronicle and the low, steady hum of a forty-year-old Goss Community press warming its rollers in the back room. [PAUSE 1.5s] Margaret Callahan stood at the light table with a grease pencil in one hand and a single sheet of county water-authority paper in the other. [LEAN IN] She was fifty-eight years old, had ink permanently stained into the cuticle of her left thumb, and had spent thirty-one years running a twice-weekly newspaper in a town of nine thousand four hundred people. [BEAT] The paper had survived two recessions, a flood in ninety-six, and the departure of the rail spur. It had twelve hundred paid subscribers, a three-person staff, and a checking account that rarely cleared four figures after payroll. [SLOW DOWN] On the light table beneath her palm sat a leaked quarterly groundwater test from the East Ward wellfield. [EMPHASIS: fourteen parts per billion] of trichloroethylene. The state advisory limit was five. The East Ward wellfield fed two places before it reached the river: Millbrook Elementary School and the Millbrook Foods canning plant. [PAUSE 2s] Margaret looked up at the wall clock. The press run started at midnight. If she pulled the story, she could slot in a harmless feature about the high school band's new uniforms, go home, sleep six hours, and keep her largest advertiser happy. If she ran it, Wednesday morning in Millbrook would never look the same.\n\n[SCENE SHIFT] The trouble had started six hours earlier, at five-thirty in the afternoon, when Arthur Vance walked through the front door without knocking. [SPEED UP] Arthur was sixty-two, wore a wool overcoat that smelled of cedar, and chaired the Millbrook Industrial Development Board. He was also the general manager of Millbrook Foods, which employed three hundred and forty people and bought a full-page grocery circular in the Chronicle every single Thursday. [BEAT] He did not sit down. He placed a manila folder on Margaret's desk and tapped it twice with his index finger. \"You got a copy of the October well test,\" he said. It was not a question. Margaret did not blink. \"I got a copy,\" she replied, keeping her voice level. \"The numbers are triple the threshold, Arthur. The elementary school is on that line.\" [LOWER VOICE] Arthur sighed, the patient, practiced sigh of a man used to explaining reality to people who did not sign paychecks. \"Those numbers are preliminary, Margaret. The state lab is re-testing next month. We're thirty days out from the municipal bond vote to upgrade the filtration plant. If you put a scare headline on page one tomorrow, the bond fails, the state pulls the matching grant, and corporate in Toledo freezes our expansion.\" [PAUSE 1.5s] He leaned across the scarred oak desk. [EMPHASIS: \"if you print this tomorrow, you close the cannery\"] \"Three hundred families, Margaret. You want that on your masthead?\" She looked at him for a long moment. \"I want clean water on my masthead,\" she said. \"You have until nine o'clock to give me a comment for the record.\" Arthur picked up his folder, paused at the door, and said quietly, \"Don't be a martyr in a town that can't afford one.\"\n\n[SCENE SHIFT] By ten-fifteen, the rain had turned to sleet, tapping against the front plate-glass window like gravel. [SLOW DOWN] Her two reporters\u2014young, earnest kids who worked for twelve dollars an hour and free coffee\u2014had gone home at nine. Margaret was alone with the layout boards, the smell of soy ink, and a silence that felt heavier than lead. [BEAT] She called her husband, David, who was waiting up with soup on the stove. She told him about Arthur's visit, and for a second she heard the tiredness in her own voice. \"What if he's right?\" she asked into the receiver. \"What if the re-test comes back clean, and I just panicked a whole town and cost people their shifts?\" [PAUSE 1.5s] David did not give her a speech. He just asked, \"Did the lab sign the sheet?\" \"Yes,\" she said. \"Then you're not guessing,\" he told her. \"You're reporting.\" [BEAT] When she hung up, the phone rang again before her hand left the cradle. It was the county health director, calling from his personal cell. His voice was tight, almost a whisper. \"Margaret, I heard you're running the East Ward numbers. I'm asking you\u2014as a neighbor\u2014hold it one week. Let us brief the school board privately first.\" [LOWER VOICE] Her stomach dropped. It was one thing to fight a plant manager. It was another to have the public health officer asking her to blink. She stared at the blank space on page one where the headline belonged. Her thumb ached. The clock read eleven-oh-five. [PAUSE 2s] For twenty minutes, she sat in her chair without moving, listening to the radiator hiss, wondering if courage was just another word for recklessness.\n\n[SCENE SHIFT] At eleven-twenty-five, the back door rattled. [LEAN IN] Margaret startled, then saw it was Tommy Morales, the sixty-four-year-old pressman who had been running the Goss since the Carter administration. Tommy was supposed to be off until midnight, but he always came in early to check the ink fountains. He walked past the light table, looked at the grease-penciled dummy of page one, and saw the empty hole above the fold. [BEAT] \"You're late,\" Tommy said, wiping his hands on a red shop rag. \"Arthur Vance came by,\" Margaret said. \"And the health director called. They want me to hold the water story until next week.\" Tommy stopped wiping his hands. He looked at the lab sheet, then at the wall where forty years of framed front pages hung\u2014the 1978 blizzard, the 1996 flood, the day the steel mill closed. [SLOW DOWN] \"Margaret,\" he said, \"my granddaughter is in third grade at Millbrook Elementary. She drinks from the hallway fountain every day after recess.\" [PAUSE 1.5s] He tapped the lab sheet with one knuckle. \"You think Arthur Vance's kids drink from that fountain?\" Margaret shook her head. \"No. They're over in the West Ward.\" Tommy nodded once, picked up his rag, and walked toward the pressroom. Over his shoulder, he said, [EMPHASIS: \"the ink was already mixed\"] \"I've got forty-five minutes before the delivery truck leaves for the post office. Tell me what to plate.\" [BEAT] Margaret stood up. The doubt didn't vanish\u2014it just stopped being the loudest thing in the room. She picked up her grease pencil, wrote a thirty-six-point headline across the top of page one\u2014EAST WARD WATER EXCEEDS STATE TOXIN LIMIT\u2014and carried the layout board into the back room.\n\n[SCENE SHIFT] At eleven-fifty-eight, Margaret stood beside the Goss press while Tommy locked the aluminum plates onto the cylinders. [STILLNESS] She knew what Wednesday would bring. Arthur Vance would pull his Thursday circular. The phone would ring off the hook with angry board members, scared parents, and people who preferred a comfortable lie to an inconvenient number. [PAUSE 1.5s] She looked at the green start button on the control panel. [LOWER VOICE] [EMPHASIS: a town does not die from bad news] It dies from the quiet agreements people make in the dark to pretend the water is fine. [BEAT] Tommy looked at her, his hand hovering over the switch. \"Ready, boss?\" Margaret took a breath, smelled the sharp, clean bite of fresh newsprint, and nodded. [EMPHASIS: \"start the press\"] [PAUSE 2s] The rollers caught with a low, rhythmic thud\u2014one, two, three\u2014and the first sheet of Wednesday's Chronicle slid onto the conveyor belt, wet with ink and impossible to unprint. [WARM SMILE] By noon the next day, the school district had brought in bottled water, the state had moved up the filtration grant by six months, and the Chronicle's subscription line had thirty-two new voicemails. [SLOW DOWN] Margaret never framed that front page. She just kept it on the light table for a week, right under the grease pencil, to remind herself what a newspaper was for.",
