      "annotatedScript": "[SCENE SHIFT] [LOWER VOICE] On the third Thursday of October, at six-twenty in the evening, the Orpheum Civic Stage in Providence smelled of scorched dust, wet wool, and old brass. Outside, a cold drizzle streaked the marquee glass. Inside, forty feet above the orchestra seats, Tomas Vega stood in Booth Four with his elbows on a scarred oak console and three ruined gel frames lined up beside his thermos. [PAUSE 1.5s]\n\nTomas was fifty-two, a theater lighting designer who had spent twenty-eight years learning how to make a room forget its own exits. Tonight was not a play. Tonight was the opening plenary of the Atlantic Design Forum\u2014nine hundred architects, founders, and civic planners who had spent the afternoon in fluorescent breakout rooms and were now, by every metric Tomas knew, [EMPHASIS: completely unreachable].\n\nHis job was simple on paper: run the house rig for the keynote speaker, a behavioral economist named Dr. Nadia Okafor, who had exactly forty minutes to land a thesis about urban attention. [LEAN IN] But Tomas had a problem that no cue sheet could fix. Three of the primary ellipsoidal instruments\u2014the ones aimed directly at the downstage edge where a speaker stands in the first ninety seconds\u2014had chewed through their amber-gold gel frames during the afternoon tech check. Someone had swapped in higher-wattage lamps without changing the heat shields. The thin polycarbonate sheets were warped, blistered, and blackened at the center like toast.\n\n[BEAT] Without those three frames, the opening wash would be raw, flat, surgical white\u2014the exact color of an airport terminal at midnight. [SLOW DOWN] And Tomas knew, better than anyone in the building, that a speaker who steps into flat white light in front of a restless crowd loses the room before she finishes her first sentence.\n\n[SCENE SHIFT] [SPEED UP] Tomas grabbed his headset and jogged down the iron spiral stairs to the fly-rail catwalk. He needed replacements\u2014Bastard Amber, Roscolux 02, or anything warm enough to pull the eye away from nine hundred glowing phone screens. He tore open the road-case drawers behind stage right. Empty, except for two strips of deep theatrical blue and a torn sheet of frost.\n\n\"Hey!\" Tomas called out to Mara, the house master electrician, who was coiling feeder cable near the loading dock. \"Mara, tell me you have a fresh book of R02 in the cage.\"\n\nMara didn't look up right away. She finished cinching a Velcro strap, wiped her palms on her jeans, and shook her head. \"Cage was cleaned out Tuesday for the touring musical. What's in the booth is what's in the building.\"\n\n\"I have three burned-out gel frames on the front truss,\" Tomas said, his voice tight. \"If I run those lamps open-white, Okafor is going to look like she's standing under a grocery store aisle. Give me anything\u2014amber, straw, even a pale rose.\"\n\nMara walked over, looked at the blackened frames in his hand, and let out a short, dry laugh. \"Tomas, it's a keynote, not Macbeth. They're here for charts. Run the house wash at sixty percent and let her talk.\"\n\n\"They're not here for charts,\" Tomas said. [EMPHASIS: \"They're here because they're tired, and if we don't change the air in the first sixty seconds, they're going to spend forty minutes checking their email.\"]\n\nMara shrugged and pointed toward the lobby doors, where the first wave of attendees was already shaking out umbrellas. \"Doors open in twenty. You've got three dead frames and no time to drive to Boston. Figure it out.\"\n\n[SCENE SHIFT] [LOWER VOICE] Tomas climbed back up to Booth Four. His knees ached. His shirt was damp at the collar. Below him, the auditorium was filling fast\u2014a low, rising tide of chatter, clinking water bottles, and the unmistakable murmur of people who had already decided they were going to be bored. [PAUSE 1.5s]\n\nHe sat down at the console and stared at the three blistered frames. For a moment, he considered doing what Mara said: bringing up the general wash, flooding the stage in safe, even light, and letting the speaker fight for the room on her own. It was the sensible choice. Nobody fires a lighting designer for a bright stage.\n\n[BEAT] Then he looked at the clock on the wall. Six-fifty-two. Eight minutes to curtain. He thought about Dr. Okafor waiting in the green room, rehearsing a brilliant opening line that would die in the air if the room stayed at seventy-five degrees and flat white. [STILLNESS] He picked up one of the burned-out frames and held it up to the tiny goose-neck lamp on his console. The center was scorched opaque, a jagged black eye. Around the edges, though, a thin ring of amber still caught the bulb.\n\n[SCENE SHIFT] [LEAN IN] A knock on the booth door broke his focus. It was Dr. Okafor herself, wearing a charcoal blazer, holding a single index card. She looked down through the glass at the restless crowd, then at the three ruined frames on the desk.\n\n\"Tell me the truth,\" she said quietly. \"Am I walking into a firing squad?\"\n\nTomas didn't sugarcoat it. \"My three front amber frames melted at four o'clock. All I have left are deep blue, frost, and raw white. If I give you raw white, you're going to have to shout over nine hundred people.\"\n\nOkafor looked at the frames, then back at the auditorium. \"What if you don't give me white at all?\"\n\nTomas blinked. \"Excuse me?\"\n\n\"You have deep blue,\" she said, pointing to the scrap of gel he'd brought up from stage right. \"And you have a blackout button. [EMPHASIS: Nobody listens in a lit room, Tomas.] Every speaker walks out into bright lights, waves, says 'Good evening, how's everybody doing?' and gives the audience permission to keep scrolling. What if we don't do that?\"\n\n[PAUSE 1.5s] Tomas stared at her. Then he looked at his console. An idea\u2014reckless, simple, and completely off the cue sheet\u2014clicked into place. \"If I drop the house to zero,\" he said slowly, \"and I put a single deep-blue edge on the lectern, you'll be a silhouette for the first thirty seconds.\"\n\n\"Good,\" Okafor said, a small smile crossing her face. \"Give me thirty seconds of blue. When I say the word 'quiet,' bring up the warm side-light\u2014just the frost, no front white. Let them lean forward to see me.\"\n\n\"And if the organizers panic?\"\n\n\"They won't,\" she said, turning toward the stairs. \"They'll be too busy listening.\"\n\n[SCENE SHIFT] [SLOW DOWN] At seven-oh-two, the house manager gave the clearance. Nine hundred people were in their seats, still talking, still glowing with phone screens. Tomas placed his fingers on the grand master fader. He didn't fade it slowly. He pulled it straight down. [PAUSE 2s]\n\nThe Orpheum dropped into total, velvet dark. A collective gasp rippled through the orchestra. Phones snapped shut\u2014not out of politeness, but because in a pitch-black room, a glowing screen feels like a flare. [LOWER VOICE] For four full seconds, Tomas held the blackout. No music. No intro voiceover. Just the sound of nine hundred people suddenly aware of their own breathing.\n\nThen Tomas bumped Channel 14\u2014the deep theatrical blue. A single, narrow blade of indigo cut across the stage floor. Dr. Okafor walked into it without a word, stopped at the center mark, and stood completely still. [BEAT] She didn't wave. She didn't thank the sponsors. She waited until the last whisper in the balcony died.\n\n\"Before you look at a single slide,\" she said, her voice low and unhurried, \"I want you to notice what just happened to your shoulders.\" [PAUSE 1.5s]\n\nUp in Booth Four, Tomas exhaled. When she hit her third sentence\u2014the exact beat she had promised\u2014he brought up the frost side-light, carving her out of the dark in soft, warm relief. [WARM SMILE] Not a single person in the room reached for a phone for the next forty minutes. And when the applause finally broke at the end, Tomas looked down at the three burned-out gel frames beside his thermos, realizing that the best open he had ever lit was the one he had been forced to build out of nothing.",
