      "annotatedScript": "[SCENE SHIFT] [LEAN IN] On the third Thursday of October in Millbrook, Iowa, the rain came down sideways against the plate-glass window of *The Millbrook Weekly*, rattling the brass mail slot like an impatient fist. [BEAT] Inside, the air smelled of damp wool, burnt coffee, and the sharp, metallic tang of offset ink. Ruthie Halvorsen, sixty-one years old, with reading glasses perched halfway down her nose and a smudge of cyan on her left cuff, stood over the light table with a grease pencil in her hand.\n\n[SLOW DOWN] Every Thursday at four o'clock, the town of four thousand two hundred people held its breath for the *Weekly*. Not because Millbrook was a city of scandals, but because Ruthie's paper was the only mirror the county owned. If a culvert washed out on County Road 9, Ruthie printed the exact depth of the ditch. If the high school band needed new uniforms, she ran the bake-sale tally on page three. [PAUSE 1.5s] Her routine never varied: final galley proofs at two, press check at three-thirty, bundles tied with orange twine by five.\n\n[SPEED UP] Except at two-fifteen that Thursday, the front page wasn't about a culvert or a bake sale. Across the top of column one sat a twelve-point headline Ruthie had typeset herself: *\"State Lab Finds Elevated Manganese in East Ward Wells.\"* Below it sat the number that had kept her awake for three nights: [EMPHASIS: forty-two parts per billion]\u2014nearly four times the state advisory threshold, pulled from six residential taps along Elm and Sycamore.\n\n[LOWER VOICE] At two-twenty, the bell above the door jangled. In walked Dale Mercer, owner of Mercer Implement & Supply, the paper's largest advertiser, followed two paces behind by Mayor Tom Keegan. Neither man shook his umbrella on the mat. Water pooled on the linoleum.\n\n\"Ruthie,\" Keegan said, his voice pitched to the false warmth of a man who had already decided how the conversation would end. \"We heard you're running the state lab sheet today.\"\n\nRuthie didn't look up from the light table. \"Lab confirmed it Tuesday, Tom. Public works got the same fax.\"\n\n\"And public works is flushing the lines Monday,\" Keegan snapped, stepping closer. \"You print that headline tonight, you're going to panic three hundred families before the weekend. Half of them will be boiling water they don't need to boil, and the other half will be calling my office.\"\n\n[PAUSE 1.5s] Dale Mercer didn't raise his voice. He simply laid a folded yellow invoice on the edge of the light table\u2014the quarterly insertion order for Mercer Implement's full-page tractor spread. \"Ruthie, my spring clearance starts Saturday. Nobody drives into town to buy a mower if the front page says the tap water's poisoned. Hold it a week. Let Tom flush the mains.\"\n\n[STILLNESS] Ruthie finally looked up. Through the rain-streaked glass, she could see the brick facade of the elementary school two blocks east. The drinking fountains in the west wing fed off the East Ward main. [BEAT] \"The lab didn't say it's poisoned, Dale,\" she said evenly. \"It said forty-two parts per billion. And the state advisory says infants and pregnant women shouldn't drink it.\"\n\n\"It's a preliminary sample!\" Keegan barked. \"You're a four-page weekly, Ruthie, not the *Des Moines Register*. You don't have a legal department if the town council decides you caused a run on the grocery store.\"\n\n[SCENE SHIFT] By five o'clock, the shop was empty except for Ruthie and Leo, her pressman, who stood by the Heidelberg offset with his arms crossed. The rain had turned to a cold drizzle. Ruthie's hands were shaking\u2014not from anger, though she was angry, but from the quiet arithmetic of a small-town ledger. Mercer's quarterly ad paid for the newsprint. Without it, she had enough cash in the checking account for three more issues, maybe four.\n\n[LOWER VOICE] Leo looked at the clock on the wall. \"Ruthie,\" he said gently. \"If we don't lock the plates in twenty minutes, the carrier won't make the rural route before dark. [EMPHASIS: The press does not wait for conscience.] Tell me what you want on page one.\"\n\nRuthie walked to the back desk and picked up the phone. She dialed the state Department of Public Health in Des Moines, hoping\u2014almost praying\u2014that the lab tech would tell her there had been a calibration error, a transposed decimal, anything that would let her pull the story with her integrity intact. [PAUSE 2s] The line rang six times before a recording clicked on: *\"Our offices are closed for the day.\"*\n\n[SLOW DOWN] She hung up. The silence in the back room was heavy, broken only by the hum of the fluorescent tube overhead. Every instinct of self-preservation told her to slide the manganese story to next Thursday. One week. Nobody would blame her. In fact, half the town would thank her.\n\n[LEAN IN] Then she thought about the three hundred families on Elm and Sycamore. She thought about Mrs. Gable, who mixed her grandson's formula with tap water every morning at six. [BEAT] If Ruthie waited a week, Mrs. Gable would pour seven more days of East Ward water into that bottle, and *The Millbrook Weekly* would have traded a child's safety for a tractor ad.\n\n[SCENE SHIFT] At five-fifteen, Ruthie walked back to the light table. Leo hadn't moved. She picked up the grease pencil, drew a clean, sharp box around the state lab's phone number at the bottom of column two so mothers could call for free test kits, and slid the galley proof across the glass.\n\n\"Run it, Leo,\" she said.\n\nLeo blinked. \"The whole thing?\"\n\n[EMPHASIS: \"You don't kill a story because it's inconvenient.\"] Ruthie's voice didn't tremble. \"You kill it when it's wrong. This one isn't wrong.\"\n\n[PAUSE 1.5s] Leo nodded once, took the proof, and turned toward the press. Within three minutes, the Heidelberg began to clatter\u2014that rhythmic, iron heartbeat that had echoed down Main Street for forty years.\n\n[WARM SMILE] [SLOW DOWN] The next morning, Dale Mercer pulled his tractor ad, just as he'd promised. Mayor Keegan didn't speak to Ruthie at the post office for three months. [BEAT] But at seven-thirty that Friday morning, before the hardware store even unlocked its doors, the elementary school principal called public works and shut off the west-wing fountains. By noon, the county health nurse had set up a bottled-water station in the church parking lot. And by Monday, six other local businesses\u2014the bakery, the lumberyard, the veterinary clinic\u2014walked into Ruthie's office and bought out every square inch of ad space Mercer had left behind.\n\n[STILLNESS] [LOWER VOICE] When Ruthie walked out to her car that Friday evening, [EMPHASIS: the ink was already wet] on the front porch of every house on Elm Street. Nobody threw a parade. Nobody gave her a medal. She just unlocked her Buick, drove home, and turned on the porch light\u2014knowing that for one Thursday in October, the mirror had not blinked. [PAUSE 2s]",
